## Bucketization and state tracking ## {#bucketization}

Changes are tracked by comparing the current catalog dump with the previous state stored in a local LevelDB instance ([`pipeline/leveldb/`](https://github.com/rdf-connect/belgium-dcat-ap-feed/tree/master/pipeline/leveldb)).
The generated activities are then bucketized using a `tree:TimebasedFragmentation` strategy with the following parameters:
- `tree:timestampPath as:published`: the property used to determine the timestamp of each activity for bucketization purposes. 
- `tree:maxSize 1000`: the maximum number of activities/events per bucket, which ensures that feed fragments remain manageable in size.
- `tree:k 4`: the branching factor of the search tree, which determines how many child buckets are created when a bucket exceeds the `maxSize` threshold.
- `tree:minBucketSpan 600` (10 minutes): the minimum time span that a bucket must cover before it can be split, which prevents excessive fragmentation of the feed when there are many events in a short period.

Bucketization state is persisted in [`pipeline/feed-state/bucketize-state.json`](https://github.com/rdf-connect/belgium-dcat-ap-feed/tree/master/pipeline/feed-state/bucketize-state.json), allowing incremental execution and reproducible feed generation across runs.

## Traversing the search tree ## {#traversing}

The feed MUST follow the chronological search tree from the Server Primer [[LDES-SERVER-PRIMER]]. 
Consequently, LDES clients SHOULD traverse the feed using the chronological ascending order mode specified in the LDES specification [[!LDES]].

# Implementation report # {#implementation}

## Architecture ## {#architecture}

The feed generation is implemented as an RDF-Connect [[!RDF_CONNECT]] pipeline defined in [`pipeline/github-pipeline.ttl`](https://github.com/rdf-connect/belgium-dcat-ap-feed/tree/master/pipeline/github-pipeline.ttl) in the source repository.
The current implementation composes six processors in sequence:
1. **`rdfc:GlobRead` (`<globReader>`)** reads the compressed DCAT data dump `../all/datagovbe_edp.xml.gz`.
2. **`rdfc:GunzipFile` (`<gunzipper>`)** decompresses the RDF/XML dump.
3. **`rdfc:DumpsToFeed` (`<asConverter>`)** converts the dump to Activity Streams events based on the stored state from previous executions.
4. **`rdfc:Sdsify` (`<sdsifier>`)** annotates members with SDS metadata which allows for stream-based fragmentation and publishing as a LDES.
5. **`rdfc:Bucketize` (`<bucketizer>`)** applies time-based fragmentation and writes state to [`./feed-state/bucketize-state.json`](https://github.com/rdf-connect/belgium-dcat-ap-feed/tree/master/pipeline/feed-state/bucketize-state.json).
6. **`rdfc:LdesDiskWriter` (`<ldesDiskWriter>`)** writes the resulting feed fragments into [`../docs/`](https://github.com/rdf-connect/belgium-dcat-ap-feed/tree/master/docs/).

The data and metadata channels are explicitly wired between processors (`<compressed_data>`, `<decompressed_data>`, `<as_stream>`, `<sds_stream>`, `<sds_metadata>`, `<bucketized_stream>`, `<bucketized_metadata>`), which ensures deterministic ordering from source dump ingestion to static feed publication.

For the GitHub Pages deployment, this pipeline is executed by [`.github/workflows/create-feed.yml`](https://github.com/rdf-connect/belgium-dcat-ap-feed/blob/master/.github/workflows/create-feed.yml) on each push to `master` and on the cron schedule `0 0 */5 * *` (every 5 days at midnight UTC).

```text
[Belgian DCAT-AP RDF/XML dump: all/datagovbe_edp.xml.gz]
         ↓
  [GlobRead]
         ↓
  [GunzipFile]
     ↓
 [DumpsToFeed Processor] → [LevelDB State]
         ↓
     [Sdsify Processor]
         ↓
 [Bucketize Processor] → [Feed State JSON]
         ↓
  [LdesDiskWriter Processor]
         ↓
 [docs/ directory] → [GitHub Pages / BOSA deployment]
```

## Setup ## {#setup}

### Prerequisites ### {#prerequisites}
- Node.js 22 or higher
- npm

### Local development ### {#local-development}

```bash
cd pipeline
npm install
npx rdfc github-pipeline.ttl
```

